### C-02.1 OpenStreetMap: open data, but no free third-party tile/API service; share-alike applies [FACT]
- Source: `https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright` — captured 2026-10-06T07:35:52Z, capture_id `2c8f5bfb1488429d91366cf9e5c3332b`, sha256 `fdbb987314529f701f516267d060fff9a64e039df63a4c982335a1a102515a6a`, 21,314 bytes, exact HTTP body.
- Locators (page sections): "OpenStreetMap licensing" — OSM data is licensed under ODbL; "You are free to copy, distribute, transmit and adapt our data, as long as you credit OpenStreetMap and its contributors. If you alter or build upon our data, you may distribute the result only under the same license." "How to credit" — attribution notice must be displayed and ODbL availability made clear. "Additional services" — "Although OpenStreetMap is open data, we cannot provide a free-of-charge map API or map tiles for third-parties," linking to the OSMF Tile/API/Nominatim usage policies. The page also notes per-country contributor attributions (e.g., OS (UK), IGN (ES), LINZ (NZ)) that may attach additional notice duties.
- The tile-policy page `https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/tiles/` was **attempted twice and failed with OSError** in this environment (recorded honestly; not used as evidence). The copyright page's own statement stands as the verified fact.
- Consequence [INFERENCE]: bundling an offline basemap by scraping the osm.org tile server is both policy-prohibited (no free third-party tiles) and untested here; the defensible route is rendering from ODbL-licensed data (planet/extracts) with attribution, or another source with an explicitly verified license. [UNCERTAIN] Whether a *derived* bundled basemap triggers ODbL's share-alike for the produced database, and how that interacts with exporting the survey team's observation tables, was **not resolved** in this stage (no legal analysis, no ODbL text captured). This is a critical unresolved dependency: the actual selected source's license must be read from its primary text before any bundle ships. A bounded alternative if share-alike is judged burdensome: keep the basemap as a separate, unmodified, attributed layer rather than merging it into project data.

### C-02.2 EPSG:4326 axis order is latitude-first; "WGS 84" is an ensemble, not a pinned datum [FACT]
- Source: `https://epsg.io/4326` — captured 2026-10-06T07:36:36Z, capture_id `f68f7e03eb89449c8a9369e492cec8e3`, sha256 `5ecbb85dbaa12f96a6268fa9f24e89baf8882ced904dcf1f324ee4e05ffbbed5`, 64,887 bytes (first 32,768 delivered).
- Locators: Attributes — "Coordinate system: Ellipsoidal 2D CS. Axes: latitude, longitude. Orientations: north, east. UoM: degree"; "Unit: degree (supplier to define representation)"; "Revision date: 2022-11-29"; "WGS84 bounds: −180.0 −90.0 / 180.0 90.0". OGC WKT2 export — `AXIS["geodetic latitude (Lat)",north,ORDER[1]]`, `AXIS["geodetic longitude (Lon)",east,ORDER[2]]`, `ENSEMBLE["World Geodetic System 1984 ensemble", MEMBER ... (Transit) ... (G2296), ENSEMBLEACCURACY[2.0]]`.
- Scope note: epsg.io is a MapTiler-operated viewer of the IOGP EPSG dataset, i.e. a secondary display of registry data. [UNEXECUTED lead] verify against the IOGP registry primary text before final ship.
- Consequences [INFERENCE]: (a) axis order is an attribute of the CRS definition, and many consumer stacks present lon/lat regardless — an import path must read axis order from the CRS definition (or force an explicit, recorded user choice when metadata is absent), not from convention; (b) even "WGS 84" carries ensemble ambiguity (multiple realizations, 2.0 m ensemble accuracy) — so a project claiming meter-grade provenance must record the realization where it matters, or state that it does not; (c) the ±180°/±90° domain bounds are registry-defined, which makes antimeridian-crossing tracks a genuine discontinuity case (the brief's suspicion is supported), not an invented universal behavior.

### C-02.3 SQLite R*Tree: candidate reduction, 32-bit coordinate rounding, and a hard read/write restriction [FACT]
- Source: `https://www.sqlite.org/rtree.html` ("The SQLite R*Tree Module", page last-modified header 2026-09-11) — captured 2026-10-06T07:36:35Z, capture_id `9390f31d33734d9db4fc6ead20d99ab1`, sha256 `de560a2eda272b455958d5e08cc2aebc0cd7d60b0a5edb3582f022812294cd55`, 41,813 bytes (first 32,768 delivered).
- Locators: §1 Overview — R-tree "does not normally provide the exact answer but merely reduces the set of potential answers" (stated as "Key Point" in §4); §3.1 — first column is a 64-bit integer PK, coordinate pairs are enforced 32-bit float (`rtree`) or 32-bit int (`rtree_i32`); §3.4 Roundoff Error — stored boxes round outward, so **contained-within queries must expand the query box (~0.000012%)** or can miss boundary entries; §3.5 — modifying the R-tree mid-query fails with `SQLITE_LOCKED`; collect results first, then update; §4.1 — auxiliary columns exist since SQLite 3.24.0 (2018-06-04), ≤100 columns, constraints on them ignored; §7.2 — `rtreecheck()` SQL function for integrity checking.
- Consequences [INFERENCE] for the 250k-observation viewport target: (a) an R*Tree gives bounded viewport access as a *filter*, and the exactness/precision contract must live in the authoritative coordinate table (full precision), with the R*Tree as a derived, rebuildable index — which also answers cache invalidation: rebuild the index from the table on project open if `rtreecheck()` or a schema/version stamp disagrees; (b) near-boundary observations (the brief's edge cases) must not be filtered by the index alone; the query-box expansion rule is mandatory; (c) viewport scan → collect rowids → mutate must be the edit loop's shape, or the UI will hit `SQLITE_LOCKED`. [UNEXECUTED] The 4-core/8 GB/250k performance figure remains an engineering target with **no measurement** in this stage; do not promote it to a benchmark claim.

### C-02.4 SQLite WAL: what "committed" means, and why relocating project media has a defined correct procedure [FACT]
- Source: `https://www.sqlite.org/wal.html` ("Write-Ahead Logging", last-modified 2026-09-11) — captured 2026-10-06T07:36:35Z, capture_id `9a2c5f36176243658b4dfecc9077014c`, sha256 `f3467b530b883d4a00574fe1a898b3d121ed72764ae28cf66941080ac0badb9e`, 42,143 bytes (delivered in two reads: bytes 0–32,768 and 32,768–42,143; range sha256 of the tail `86e11deb...`).
- Locators: §2 How WAL Works — "A COMMIT occurs when a special record indicating a commit is appended to the WAL." §2.3 — with `PRAGMA synchronous=NORMAL` only checkpoints sync, so "transactions are no longer durable and might rollback following a power failure or hard reset"; FULL syncs every commit. §4 The WAL File — "If a database file is separated from its WAL file, then transactions that were previously committed to the database might be lost, or the database file might become corrupted. The only safe way to remove a WAL file is to open the database file using one of the sqlite3_open() interfaces then immediately close the database using sqlite3_close()." §10 — WAL-mode DBs carry header version 2 (bytes 18/19); pre-3.7.0 SQLite cannot recover them. §9 — `SQLITE_BUSY` can surface during crash recovery by the first new connection.
- Consequences [INFERENCE] for the brief's "relocating project media" and "interrupted save" requirements: (a) copy/move/export of a project must be defined as **checkpoint + clean close, then copy the single DB file** (or copy `-wal`/`-shm` together — but the checkpoint-then-copy rule is simpler and provably safe per the cited text); a naive file copy while open is a defined corruption/loss path, not an edge case; (b) "committed vs incomplete work" maps cleanly onto WAL commit records — after a crash, reopening runs recovery, and the app should surface `SQLITE_BUSY` during that window rather than reporting failure; (c) choose `synchronous=FULL` for the field tool's save path (battery pull is a realistic field event) — [CHOICE], cost accepted.

