## C-03. Real issue → fix → regression chain, verified this stage

Two chains were verified (the brief asks for at least one). Both bear directly on this product's riskiest areas: silent axis-order transposition and local database corruption.

### C-03.1 PROJ: EngineeringCRS axis order silently transposed (issue #4876, fix proposed in PR #4877, **unreleased** as of capture) [FACT]
- Source: GitHub API search `repo:OSGeo/PROJ "axis order" in:title` — captured 2026-10-06T07:37:35Z, capture_id `2be2c2306f3e494ab8a78492a13fbcbd`, sha256 `74469e1e0045e53d284d48abadc31a710af4311a9db1b168b6e8d35d448b22c7`, 22,782 bytes, status 200. Items used (all data quoted from the API response body):
  - **Issue #4876** (state open, created 2026-10-01, label `bug`): "`proj_normalize_for_visualization()` does not normalise an EngineeringCRS, and Cartesian Grid Offsets ignores its axis order." Body states environment **PROJ 9.8.1 and master as of 2026-10-01, pyproj 3.8.0, EPSG v12.029 (in PROJ 9.8.1)**, with reproductions: a plant grid declared (northing, easting) keeps that order under `always_xy=True` (contradicting the documented behavior), and EPSG:9656 Cartesian Grid Offsets adds the easting offset to the northing — coordinates come out transposed **with no error**. Numbers from the body: point 100 m east / 200 m north of origin (478000, 1078000) yields `(478100, 1078200)` in one mode and `(478200, 1078100)` in the other, depending on method and normalization mode. Body notes the same class was previously fixed for derived projected CRSs (#3477) and bound CRSs (#3598), i.e. the bug class recurs per CRS type.
  - **PR #4877** (state open, `merged_at: null`, created 2026-10-01, "Fixes #4876"): normalizes EngineeringCRS with CartesianCS and adapts Cartesian Grid Offsets export; lists regression tests in `test_crs.cpp`, `test_operation.cpp`, and `test_c_api.cpp` (reproduction to 1e-9 m in both modes, plus a CIG85 (east, north) regression check). **Not merged at capture time.**
  - Corroborating merged precedent: **PR #3826** "proj_factors(): make it work with projected CRS with non-metre unit and/or northing/easting axis order (fixes #3824)", merged 2023-07-19T17:35:50Z, milestone 9.3.0.
- Release/branch applicability: defect 1 and 2 are present in **PROJ 9.8.1 and master as of 2026-10-01** per the issue body; the fix is **not in any release** at capture time. An issue title alone wouldn't prove product-wide correctness — here the issue body's own reproduction and the PR's test plan are the evidence, and both being *open* means any current PROJ-based import path inherits the silent transposition risk for engineering/local grid CRSs.
- Design consequence [INFERENCE]: this is precisely the brief's "displayed pin is not proof the exported data kept its location." A field tool that ingests local/site grids (plausibly engineering CRSs) must not rely on transform-library normalization alone: validate axis order at import from the CRS definition (per C-02.2), require a bounded user confirmation when the definition conflicts with the column header, and store the CRS WKT/ID + transform identity in record provenance. Pin the PROJ version per project and re-verify transforms when the pin changes [proposed validation, UNEXECUTED].

### C-03.2 SQLite "WAL-reset bug": corruption window, fix in 3.51.3, backports published [FACT]
- Source: same wal.html capture as C-02.4, §11 "The WAL-Reset Bug" / §11.1 / §11.2 (page states last update 2026-08-25).
- Verified chain: bug found and fixed 2026-03-03 by an SQLite developer; "likely present in all versions of SQLite from 3.7.0 (2010-07-21) through 3.51.2 (2026-01-09)"; **fixed in 3.51.3 (2026-03-13)**; backports: **3.44.6** (`sqlite.org/src/info/863c171b76cd36e0`) and **3.50.7** (`sqlite.org/src/info/c7facf7ac58d2cda`). Conditions: WAL mode with **two or more connections in separate threads/processes** attempting write/checkpoint at the same instant; race leaves a wal-index header field wrong so a later checkpoint skips part of a committed transaction → "the database file goes corrupt." Developers could not reproduce organically and added special testing logic via `sqlite3_test_control()` to verify the fix (§11.2); an independent reproducer was published 2026-08-24 (linked from the page; not independently fetched this stage).
- Release/branch applicability: any build ≤3.51.2 carries the latent race for multi-connection WAL use; fixed line 3.51.3+ or the two backport branches. The fix's own verification is a purpose-built regression trigger inside SQLite's test suite — an isolated passing test there does not prove any application's whole-database correctness, but it is the upstream project's own fix evidence.
- Design consequence [INFERENCE] + [CHOICE]: a field tool on tablets/laptops will run background readers (map viewport) and a writer (save). Either pin SQLite ≥ 3.51.3 (or a backport branch) as a hard dependency floor — preferred — or architect for a single serialized connection so the race window is closed by construction. This must appear in the proposal's critical-dependencies section; it was very unlikely to be discovered without following the doc's bug section.

